    {
      "datasource": {
        "type": "postgres",
        "uid": "dropset-postgres"
      },
      "description": "How each leg's sources resolved, latest per market and leg. This is what replaced per-venue attribution: a leg is a candidate set, so there is no single answering feed to name.\n\nRead `consensus_state` first. `Corroborated` (3+ agreeing) and `Agreed` (2 agreeing) are healthy. `SingleTrusted` is one source designated believable alone. `SingleUnverified` is one source with nothing corroborating it \u2014 for most of this roster that is the STEADY STATE, not a fault, and it is the only signal that a market is quoted off one unchecked feed; a market appearing here that the coverage survey did not predict is a genuine signal. `Dispersed` means the sources disagreed beyond the band, and then `dispersion_outlier` names the source furthest from the consensus \u2014 the SUSPECT to distrust, never 'the feed that answered'.\n\nA leg missing from this table resolved to nothing: either no source offered a reading, or every candidate was too stale. Which source went quiet is the feed-health table, which is per source rather than per leg.",
